course: religion-ethics
lesson: 36
title: What is an ethics olympiad?
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ethics means thinking carefully about what is right and wrong, good and bad, and above all why: it is about reasons, not just opinions.
explain: In an ethics olympiad, school teams are given short stories called cases, each with a dilemma, where every choice has something costly about it.
explain: The teams do not win by shouting the other side down; judges give points for clear reasons, understanding the case, thinking about every person affected, answering questions well and showing respect.
explain: In many rounds one team gives its view and the other team responds, adding ideas and asking questions rather than attacking.
explain: For a child today this is good practice for real life: at school or at home you can slow down, ask who is affected, listen, and give your reasons kindly, which is also what Jesus' command to love your neighbour asks of a Christian.
fact: Ethics is careful thinking about right and wrong, good and bad, and the reasons for them.
fact: An opinion just says what you think; a reasoned view also says why, so others can weigh it.
fact: An ethics olympiad is a competition where school teams discuss real dilemmas.
fact: Teams are judged on how well they think and listen, not on who "wins" the argument.
fact: The Ethics Bowl is a similar competition in the USA and elsewhere, where teams analyse cases and respond to each other.
fact: Unlike a debate, an ethics bowl does not give teams a side they must defend; they may agree with each other.
fact: A dilemma is a situation where every choice has something wrong or costly about it.
fact: A case is a short story given to the teams to discuss, before or during the competition.
fact: Stakeholders are everyone affected by a decision, including people and animals who are not in the room.
fact: Judges look for clear reasons, understanding of the case, thinking about other views, good answers to questions, and respect.
fact: In commentary and response, the second team builds on the first team's ideas and raises questions, rather than attacking.
fact: Teams prepare together, share the speaking and support each other.
exercise: What is ethics? | Thinking carefully about right and wrong and the reasons why | Learning all the rules of a game so that you can always win it | Saying your opinion loudly until others agree with it | Memorising a list of things that are banned at school
exercise: What is the difference between an opinion and a reasoned view? | A reasoned view also gives the reasons why you think it | A reasoned view is just said in a louder and surer voice | An opinion is always wrong and a reasoned view always right | There is no difference; they are two words for one thing
exercise: Why are olympiad teams marked for listening as well as speaking? | Good thinking means understanding others before you answer | Judges get bored if one team talks for too long | Listening gives the other team less time to score points | Teams that listen are allowed to skip the judges' questions
exercise: In an ethics olympiad, how do teams get points? | By giving clear reasons and treating others with respect | By talking faster and for longer than the other team does | By making the other team look silly in front of judges | By choosing the answer that most people already like
exercise: How is an ethics bowl different from a debate? | Teams are not forced onto opposite sides and can agree | Teams must always argue the opposite of what they think | Only one team is allowed to speak in each round | The team with the loudest speaker always wins it
exercise: What is a dilemma? | A situation where every choice has something costly about it | A question with one easy answer that everybody already knows | A game in which two teams try to trick each other | A story where the hero always does the right thing
exercise: Make up a dilemma from school life. Which one is a real dilemma? | Your friend copied homework; telling could hurt them, staying quiet is unfair | You can choose a red or a blue pencil and both work fine | You find your own lunchbox and decide to eat your lunch | The bell rings and you go back to class with everyone
exercise: What is "the case" in an ethics olympiad? | A short story given to teams to discuss | The box where the judges keep the prizes | The rule book every team must learn by heart | The final score at the end of the competition
exercise: What should you ask first when you read a case? | What is happening, and who is affected by the choice? | Which answer will make our team sound the cleverest? | How can we finish talking about it as fast as we can? | Which of the people in the story is the worst one?
exercise: Who are the stakeholders in a decision? | Everyone affected by it, even people not in the room | Only the people who make the decision themselves, nobody else | Only the grown-ups who are in charge of the place at the time | Only the people who happen to be in the room at the time
exercise: A school cuts its playtime. Which list of stakeholders is the most complete? | Pupils, teachers, parents and the lunchtime staff | Only the head teacher who made the decision | Only the pupils in the youngest class of the school | Only the children who like football at playtime
exercise: What makes an answer impressive to an olympiad judge? | Clear reasons, care for other views and respect | A long speech with lots of very difficult words | Never changing your mind whatever anyone says | Picking the answer the judge seems to like best
exercise: In commentary and response, what does the second team do? | Builds on the first team's ideas and raises questions | Tries to prove the first team are not very clever at all | Repeats the first team's words again but much more loudly | Stays silent and waits for the judges to decide the round
exercise: How can a team make sure everyone gets to speak? | Plan who speaks on which part and invite quiet members in | Let the most confident member do all of the talking | Speak all at once so that nobody is ever left out of the round | Choose the speaker by whoever shouts first in the round
exercise: Which matters most in an ethics olympiad? | How well you think, not which answer you pick | Which answer you pick, not how you got to it | How many long words you can fit into a speech | How quickly you can give an answer to the case
test: My big brother says the ethics olympiad is just arguing and the loudest team wins. What would you tell him? | No, judges mark reasons, listening and respect, not loudness | Yes, the team that shouts the longest always gets the points | Yes, but only in the last round when the judges are tired | No, the winners are picked by drawing names from a hat
test: Your classmate Amir, who is Muslim, asks: "Can I be in the olympiad team if I'm not Christian?" What is the best answer? | Yes, it is about careful thinking, and anyone of any faith or none can join | No, only people from one religion can think well about right and wrong | Yes, but he must agree with the Christians on every single case | No, ethics olympiads are only for people who go to church each week
test: ugh why we gotta talk about who's "affected", just say what's right innit | Because a good answer weighs everyone the choice touches | Because judges like it when you say long lists of people's names | Because the case is boring and it fills up the time | Because thinking about others means you have no view
test: At practice, Zoe says: "Our answer is right because I said so, end of." What is wrong with this? | She gives no reason others can weigh, so it is not a reasoned view | Nothing, because a confident answer is always the strongest answer | She should have said it more loudly so judges hear it | She should have said a famous person said it instead
test: Which of these is a dilemma? | Keeping a friend's secret that they are being hurt, or telling a teacher | Deciding whether to wear your blue socks or your other blue socks | Choosing to eat a biscuit you like when nobody else wants it | Going home after school by the same road you always take
test: The other team just said something good. What is the best olympiad response? | "We agree with that and want to add one more point" | "That is wrong, and you clearly did not read the case" | "We are not going to answer that, it's your problem" | "Whatever, our team is cleverer than your team anyway"
test: A judge asks: "Who else might be affected that you didn't mention?" What is the best reply? | Think again and name people or animals left out, like the neighbours | Say the judge is wrong, because you have already said everyone important | Say that only the main person in the story really matters at all | Refuse to answer because it was not part of your planned speech
test: shut up Leo, ur not even talking in the round, just let me do it. Which olympiad idea does this break? | Teamwork: teams share the speaking and support each other | Stakeholders: everyone affected must be in the room to speak | Dilemmas: every choice must be easy and costless | Cases: the story must be read out by one person
test: The school wants to close the library to make a car park. Who is a stakeholder you might forget? | Next year's pupils who will never get to use the library | The person who painted the lines on the old car park | The author of the rule book of the ethics olympiad in America | A child in a school in another country far away
test: Which shows a reasoned view, not just an opinion? | "Pets in class are a bad idea because some children are allergic" | "Pets in class are a bad idea and that is just what I think" | "Pets in class are a bad idea, trust me, I know it" | "Pets in class are a bad idea, everyone says so"
test: My little sister says ethics is just being nice. Put her right. | Ethics is thinking about what is right and why, which can mean hard choices | Ethics is a school subject where you learn to be quiet in class | Ethics is a list of rules that you learn and never think about | Ethics is only for grown-ups, so children do not need to bother
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the criteria upon which adjudicators of an ethics olympiad confer marks? | The clarity of reasoning, consideration of other views and respectful conduct | The volume of the speaker's voice and the duration of the presentation | The number of the opposing team's arguments which are ridiculed | The popularity of the conclusion with members of the audience
exam: Identify the definition of a "stakeholder" consistent with the established usage of the competition. | Every party affected by a decision, whether present or not | Solely the person who makes the decision in question, and no other | Solely those persons physically present in the room at the time | Solely the adults having authority over the matter under discussion
exam: Select the response that most accurately characterises a dilemma. | A situation in which every available choice carries some cost or wrong | A question admitting of one obvious answer known to all | A contest in which teams endeavour to deceive one another | A narrative in which the hero invariably acts rightly
exam: Whereas in a formal debate sides are assigned, ascertain the feature which distinguishes the Ethics Bowl. | Teams are not compelled to oppose one another and may concur | Teams are compelled always to oppose their own true opinion on the case | One team alone is permitted to speak in every round | The victory is invariably awarded to the loudest speaker
